h, cycle, star, complete, 2D grid, binary tree, lollipop, random tree, Erdos-Renyi, random 3-regular (5 <= n <= 30)\"\n649\t      ],\n650\t      \"central_topic\": \"Empirical prediction of the expected cover time of simple random walks on small connected graphs (n <= 30) from a few cheap graph statistics (n, m, degrees, diameter), compared against classical worst-case cover-time bounds, with per-family error and Monte Carlo uncertainty.\",\n651\t      \"comparators\": [\n652\t        \"Aleliunas-Karp-Lipton-Lovasz-Rackoff bound 2m(n-1)\",\n653\t        \"Feige tight upper bound (4/27) n^3\",\n654\t        \"Matthews bound: max hitting time times harmonic number\",\n655\t        \"electrical-network / commute-time characterisation of cover time\",\n656\t        \"constant and OLS log-linear reference predictors\"\n657\t      ],\n658\t      \"core_methods\": [\n659\t        \"log-linear ridge regression of Monte Carlo cover-time estimates on log graph statistics (n, m, mean/max/min degree, diameter, log n * log diameter)\",\n660\t        \"Monte Carlo estimation of uniform-start expected cover time with standard errors\",\n661\t        \"exact maximum hitting time computation for the Matthews reference bound\"\n662\t      ],\n663\t      \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n664\t        \"queries phrased as the research question itself (e.g. 'predict cover time from graph statistics small graphs regression') that could surface the forbidden original paper or pages citing it\",\n665\t        \"generic machine learning / benchmarking / optimisation queries (e.g. 'regression model evaluation', 'cross-validation')\",\n666\t        \"graph neural network or learned graph-embedding queries (not used in the run)\",\n667\t        \"chronology or campaign-\n668\t\n669\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n670\t```\n671\t\n672\t## manuscript source excerpt\n673\t\n674\t```tex\n675\t\\documentclass{article}\n676\t\n677\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n678\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n679\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n680\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n681\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n682\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n683\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n684\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n685\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n686\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n687\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n688\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n689\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n690\t}\n691\t\n692\t \n693\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n694\t\n695\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n696\t\\usepackage{url}\n697\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n698\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n699\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n700\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n701\t\n702\t\\title{Six Cheap Statistics versus Worst-Case Bounds:\\\\Predicting Random-Walk Cover Times on Small Graphs}\n703\t\\iclrfinalcopy\n704\t\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n705\t\n706\t\\newcommand{\\Ccov}{C}\n707\t\\newcommand{\\diam}{\\mathrm{diam}}\n708\t\n709\t\\begin{document}\n710\t\\maketitle\n711\t\n712\t\\begin{abstract}\n713\tThe classical cover-time bounds $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$ hold for every graph but can be loose on concrete small graphs. We ask whether six cheap statistics, namely vertex and edge counts, mean, maximum and minimum degree, and diameter, predict the expected cover time of connected graphs with $5\\le n\\le 30$ more accurately, and how the error depends on graph type. An untuned log-linear ridge regression is fitted to Monte Carlo labels on 117 graphs from ten synthetic families. It is scored on 116 held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, with a paired two-level bootstrap. Its mean absolute log error is 0.183 (95\\% CI 0.167--0.207), a typical error factor of 1.20, against 1.397 (factor 4.0) for the better bound. The paired advantage is significant in 9 of 10 families and largest where the bounds are loosest (complete, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi, random 3-regular). On lollipops, where the cubic bound is nearly tight, the advantage is not statistically clear. Fitted prediction helps most far from the worst case, but it carries no guarantee outside the studied families and sizes.\n714\t\\end{abstract}\n715\t\n716\t\n717\t\\section{Introduction}\n718\t\\label{sec:intro}\n719\t\n720\tStart a simple random walk at a vertex of a connected undirected graph $G=(V,E)$ and let it step to a uniformly random neighbour until every vertex has been visited. The expected number of steps is the \\emph{cover time}. It governs randomized graph exploration and the space-efficient connectivity algorithm of \\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWU}, and more generally any procedure that must touch every state of a reversible Markov chain \\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO}. The theory is mature. \\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWU} showed that the cover time of any connected graph with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges is at most $2m(n-1)$. Feige later showed that the worst case over all $n$-vertex graphs is $(4/27)n^3$ to leading order, attained asymptotically by lollipop graphs (for these worst-case results see the survey of \\citealp{Lovász2001RandomWO}), and that every connected graph needs at least about $n\\ln n$ steps \\citep{Feige1995ATL}. These results are tight \\emph{over the class of all graphs}. For a specific small graph that is far from the extremal shape, both bounds can overshoot the true cover time by a large factor.\n721\t\n722\tPractitioners who need a number for a given small graph, for example to set a walk budget or sanity-check a simulator, therefore face a gap. The worst-case bounds are cheap but loose. Exact computation or long simulation is accurate but needs the whole graph and substantial compute. Tighter graph-aware bounds also need full structural information, such as all pairwise hitting times or effective resistances \\citep{Chandra1989TheER}. This motivates a narrow empirical question that the literature leaves open:\n723\t\n724\t\\emph{For connected graphs with at most 30 vertices, can a handful of easily computed statistics (vertex count, edge count, mean, maximum and minimum degree, and diameter) predict the cover time more accurately than the generic bounds? On which kinds of graph is such a predictor reliable, and where does it fail?}\n725\t\n726\tWe answer with a controlled, reproducible measurement rather than a new model. The predictor is a deliberately simple, untuned log-linear ridge regression on the logarithms of the six statistics plus one interaction term. It is fitted on Monte Carlo estimates of the uniform-start cover time. All accuracy claims come from a single sealed evaluation on held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, drawn from ten synthetic families: paths, cycles, stars, complete graphs, 2D grids, binary trees, lollipops, random trees, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs and random 3-regular graphs. Uncertainty is quantified by a two-level bootstrap that resamples both graphs and Monte Carlo walks. Comparisons with each bound use paired resamples.\n727\t\n728\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n729\t\\begin{itemize}\n730\t\\item \\textbf{A calibrated accuracy comparison with the generic bounds.} On held-out graphs, the six-statistic predictor has family-weighted mean absolute log error 0.183 (95\\% CI 0.167--0.207). The best of $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$ has error 1.397, and the paired difference has CI $[-1.303,-1.130]$ (\\S\\ref{sec:main}). A size-only fitted reference, a constant predictor and a permuted-feature control show that the gain depends on the degree and diameter statistics and on each graph's own feature vector (\\S\\ref{sec:controls}).\n731\t\\item \\textbf{A family-resolved reliability map.} We report per-family errors and paired gains with confidence intervals. The predictor is most accurate on stars, random 3-regular graphs, random trees and paths, and least accurate on complete, grid and Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi graphs. Its advantage over the bounds is significant in nine families and not statistically clear on lollipops (\\S\\ref{sec:family}).\n732\t\\item \\textbf{A mechanistic reading of where fitted prediction helps.} The per-family gain tracks the slack of the best bound. Gains are smallest on the two families near the bounds' extremal shapes (lollipop and path). They are largest on complete graphs, which attain the $n\\ln n$ lower-bound regime, and on Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi and random 3-regular graphs, where the measured bound slack is similarly large (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n733\t\\end{itemize}\n734\t\n735\t\n736\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n737\t\\label{sec:related}\n738\t\n739\t\\paragraph{Cover time and its generic bounds.}\n740\tFor a connected graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, let $\\tau_{\\mathrm{cov}}$ be the first time a simple random walk has visited every vertex. Following the evaluation protocol in \\S\\ref{sec:protocol}, we study the \\emph{uniform-start} cover time\n741\t\\begin{equation}\n742\t\\Ccov(G) \\;=\\; \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{v\\in V}\\mathbb{E}_v\\!\\left[\\tau_{\\mathrm{cov}}\\right],\n743\t\\end{equation}\n744\twhich averages over a uniformly random starting vertex. The spanning-tree argument of \\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWU} gives $\\Ccov(G)\\le 2m(n-1)$ for every start vertex. The bound overestimates dense graphs badly: for the complete graph $K_n$ it is $n(n-1)^2$, whereas $\\Ccov(K_n)=(n-1)H_{n-1}$, where $H_k$ is the $k$-th harmonic number \\citep{Lovász2001RandomWO}. Feige's upper bound $\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case over $n$-vertex graphs and is approached by lollipops, i.e.\\ a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices joined to a path; the survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO} places this result alongside the extremal hitting-time analysis of the lollipop. At the other extreme, every connected graph has cover time at least $(1-o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, which the complete graph approaches \\citep{Feige1995ATL}. The ratio between the two extremes grows like $n^2/\\ln n$. Even at $n\\le 30$, true cover times therefore span orders of magnitude across graph shapes. A bound that depends only on $n$ and $m$ cannot be accurate across that whole range.\n745\t\n746\t\\paragraph{Graph-aware characterizations.}\n747\tTighter estimates use more structure. Matthews' method, presented in the survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO}, bounds the cover time by the maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$. \\citet{Chandra1989TheER} showed that commute times equal $2m$ times the effective resistance between the endpoints. As a result, the cover time lies within a logarithmic factor of $m\\,R_{\\max}$, where $R_{\\max}$ is the largest effective resistance between any two vertices. Since $R_{\\max}$ is at most the diameter, the product $m\\cdot\\diam(G)$ is a natural cheap proxy for this resistance scale. Exact hitting times and effective resistances need a linear solve on the full adjacency matrix, so they are not ``cheap statistics'' in our sense. We still include a Matthews-type reference (maximum hitting time times $H_{n-1}$) as a graph-aware comparison point. The survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO} collects the classical closed forms for paths, cycles, complete graphs and stars. These closed forms are used here only to check the simulator (\\S\\ref{sec:protocol}).\n748\t\n749\t\\paragraph{Positioning.}\n750\tPrior work on cover time, as collected in the survey of \\citet{Lovász2001RandomWO}, is overwhelmingly analytic: it derives bounds that hold for all graphs, and closed forms or growth rates for particular families such as paths, cycles, stars and complete graphs. We do not rely on finer asymptotic results for random families; for random 3-regular, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi and grid graphs, all statements in this paper about regimes rest on our own measurements. We are not aware of a controlled, held-out measurement of how well a few global statistics predict the cover time of concrete small graphs, broken down by graph family, with Monte Carlo and graph-sampling uncertainty propagated into the comparison. Our study is complementary to the analytic results, not a substitute. Bounds come with guarantees, whereas a fitted predictor is only as good as the match between training and test distributions. We therefore treat the bounds \\emph{as predictors}, i.e.\\ we score them by how close they are to the true value, and we limit every claim to the evaluated families and sizes.\n751\t\n752\t\n753\t\\section{Method}\n754\t\\label{sec:method}\n755\t\n756\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n757\t\\centering\n758\t\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\textwidth,height=0.3\\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/overview_concept.png}\n759\t\\caption{Overview of the study. Graphs from ten synthetic families ($5\\le n\\le 30$) are reduced to six cheap statistics. A log-linear ridge regression fitted on Monte Carlo cover-time labels maps them to a predicted cover time, which is compared with the generic bounds $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$. Every predictor is scored on held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, using a paired two-level bootstrap over graphs and walks.}\n760\t\\label{fig:overview}\n761\t\\end{figure}\n762\t\n763\t\\subsection{Problem setup}\n764\t\\label{sec:setup}\n765\tThe input to a predictor is a training set of connected graphs, each described by the feature vector\n766\t\\begin{equation}\n767\tx(G) = \\bigl(n,\\; m,\\; \\bar d = 2m/n,\\; d_{\\max},\\; d_{\\min},\\; \\diam(G)\\bigr)\n768\t\\end{equation}\n769\tand a Monte Carlo estimate $\\hat \\Ccov(G)$ of its uniform-start cover time with standard error. After fitting, the predictor sees only $x(G)$ for each held-out graph. It sees no adjacency information, family label, graph identifier or held-out label, and it must return a strictly positive prediction $\\hat y(G)$. The error on one graph is the absolute log ratio, capped at $\\ln 1000$:\n770\t\\begin{equation}\n771\te(G) = \\min\\bigl(\\,|\\ln \\hat y(G) - \\ln \\hat \\Ccov(G)|,\\; \\ln 1000\\bigr).\n772\t\\end{equation}\n773\tErrors are averaged within each family, and the ten family means are averaged with equal weight. We call this family-weighted average the \\emph{mean absolute log error}; lower is better. Equal family weighting stops the large random families from dominating the score. A mean absolute log error of $\\epsilon$ means a typical multiplicative error of $e^{\\epsilon}$; we use the term geometric-mean error factor for $e^{\\epsilon}$ in this informal sense. The log scale is natural because cover times across families differ by orders of magnitude, and because over- and underestimating by the same factor should cost the same.\n774\t\n775\t\\subsection{Design rationale}\n776\t\\label{sec:rationale}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67ee5d0a-908e-40a8-a0f6-1aad3920c0a7","uuid":"fbeb207f-9a91-480a-972f-5c53a2f8f69f","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:54:40.437Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agent-home/.claude/projects/-workspace/67ee5d0a-908e-40a8-a0f6-1aad3920c0a7/tool-results/b7o1nukzk.txt","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-10-27T18:54:14.423013+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171538Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171600/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261027_182218-paper_evidence_audit_round_2-ful1cz1t/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded